# The app's Flows screen, driven by whichever adapter is pointed at it
# (T11.2, T11.4, LLD §13.9).
#
# This is the flow **both sides of the parity gate run**: the AX adapter reads
# the accessibility tree macOS publishes, and the Playwright adapter attached
# over CDP reads the DOM that tree is serialised from. Same sentences, same
# application, two oracles — and a disagreement is a finding rather than a
# flake.
#
# It stops short of quitting, and that is the point of it being a separate file:
# `Quit the app` is a desktop step and a web adapter refuses it (pattern 31), so
# a flow with it in cannot be the one both sides run. `app-lifecycle.flow` is
# the whole journey, and the parity report lists its last step as one-sided.
#
# ## The number in the file name
#
# `yam run` executes a project's `test:` blocks in file order, and one
# session serves the whole run — so a flow that ends with `Quit the app` has to
# be the last one, or every flow after it drives an application that is not
# there. `99-` says that out loud; `01-` and `02-` say the rest may run in any
# order.

story: the app opens a project and shows its flows
  The first recent project should be visible
  Click the first recent project
  The Flows rail item should be visible
  Click the Flows rail item
  # Waited for by *resolving* something the Flows screen only has with a project
  # open (T20). Opening a project starts a service, which takes seconds, and an
  # expectation is evaluated once — it is the *resolution* that retries. So the
  # step that absorbs the wait has to be one that looks for an element, and
  # `Save flow` is on no other screen. Without it the web adapter read the
  # toolbar before the project had finished opening; the AX side passed only
  # because it is slower.
  The Save flow button should be visible
  The toolbar title should contain "Flows"

test: the app screen
  the app opens a project and shows its flows
